orked-solution "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $m∠D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {135}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}+m∠D+{90}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {445}^{∘}+m∠D & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={95}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d6d5827-91a8-5fd4-85ea-7564e8f5396e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8464769e-d101-5cc6-84af-8419f4445e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104957} {:answer/id #uuid "874896de-afd0-5d26-86be-c0da5f57ef72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104958} {:answer/id #uuid "9c49e928-c7db-5b84-bae0-a98681af5056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104959} {:answer/id #uuid "07de0afd-378b-51c4-b9ed-bed37e8b7764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104960} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca84bc5-9fd1-5197-b13f-00e365f7abb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca84bc5-9fd1-5197-b13f-00e365f7abb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104961} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ef2487-e6d2-501a-8f40-6e3e3c076726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "402ba7bd-dca2-507f-827e-423295d57d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-68022" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\nI. ${64}^{∘}$, ${73}^{∘}$, ${98}^{∘}$, ${124}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${73}^{∘}$, ${112}^{∘}$, ${93}^{∘}$, ${82}^{∘}$\n\nIII. ${80}^{∘}$, ${140}^{∘}$, ${49}^{∘}$, ${84}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{64}^{∘}+{73}^{∘}+{98}^{∘}+{124}^{∘}={359}^{∘}\\,X \\\\  &  \\\\ {73}^{∘}+{112}^{∘}+{93}^{∘}+{82}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓ \\\\  &  \\\\ {80}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}+{49}^{∘}+{84}^{∘}={353}^{∘}\\,X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only option II could be the angles of a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d6d5827-91a8-5fd4-85ea-7564e8f5396e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a7898a6-14af-555e-8c57-5a5e410541d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499709} {:answer/id #uuid "357395bb-c75f-5d23-bf4d-03e608a41ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499765} {:answer/id #uuid "952bcbd0-687e-56a9-8231-132174a60417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499946} {:answer/id #uuid "b493e1f9-92d6-5393-b240-31cb7a04b42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500019} {:answer/id #uuid "eaede687-ba2c-5de9-9d44-59da03c3a825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "952bcbd0-687e-56a9-8231-132174a60417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499946} :answer-field/id #uuid "cac177c2-9ea5-5187-a8ce-ea6749eb3c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d999c138-4be3-55d4-a8c5-6e1a2cbd965f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74239" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\nI. ${41}^{∘}$, ${89}^{∘}$, ${100}^{∘}$, ${130}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${15}^{∘}$, ${119}^{∘}$, ${82}^{∘}$, ${131}^{∘}$\n\nIII. ${21}^{∘}$, ${74}^{∘}$, ${110}^{∘}$, ${155}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{41}^{∘}+{89}^{∘}+{100}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓ \\\\  &  \\\\ {15}^{∘}+{119}^{∘}+{82}^{∘}+{131}^{∘}={347}^{∘}\\,X \\\\  &  \\\\ {21}^{∘}+{74}^{∘}+{110}^{∘}+{155}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only options I and III could be the angles of a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b111120-41dd-5a58-bcc5-b0b6fc78b384" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Sides of a Polygon Given the Sum of Its Interior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fab282-cea6-54d6-9c7b-6abd288a09e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499702} {:answer/id #uuid "a1dd7d91-8551-513b-88c0-f9d5414ec035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499968} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0ddcb8-b0bf-516b-9997-05324f124420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499996} {:answer/id #uuid "cddfe483-9794-51a8-83e5-7afd6219f922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500075} {:answer/id #uuid "ddded419-4708-5c4c-98ca-3fea86e4d404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14fab282-cea6-54d6-9c7b-6abd288a09e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499702} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e8cc93-af12-5bc4-8221-a544d7de6f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be9eb345-ad38-54e4-ae93-ee7a03d3fd33" :question/math-academy-id "q-74243" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)={360}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =2 \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=4$ sides (so it is a quadrilateral)."}]}
